      <!-- Frequently Asked Questions Section (Visibly rendered matching Schema 1:1) -->
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Planning 10 Days in Morocco</h2>

      <div class="roam-faq-accordion" style="margin: 28px 0;">
        <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08); padding: 18px 0;">
          <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; color: #111;">Is 10 days in Morocco enough to see the highlights?</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Yes, 10 days is the ideal duration for a first-time trip to Morocco. It provides ample time to explore two imperial cities (Marrakech and Fes), cross the High Atlas Mountains via the Tizi n'Tichka pass, explore UNESCO kasbahs like Aït Benhaddou, and spend two nights in the Erg Chebbi Sahara Desert without exhausting transit days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08); padding: 18px 0;">
          <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; color: #111;">What is the best route for a 10-day Morocco itinerary?</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            The most efficient route is an open-jaw circuit: arrive in Casablanca or Fes, travel south through the Middle Atlas to Merzouga (Erg Chebbi dunes), continue westward through the Dades and Todra Gorges, cross the High Atlas to Marrakech, and depart from Marrakech (RAK) or return to Casablanca (CMN). This eliminates 7 to 8 hours of redundant highway driving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08); padding: 18px 0;">
          <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; color: #111;">How much does a 10-day trip to Morocco cost?</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            A mid-range independent trip typically costs $1,200 to $1,800 per person excluding flights. A private luxury chauffeur tour with dedicated English-speaking Berber driver, modern 4x4 Prado, handpicked 4-star boutique riads, and luxury Sahara glamping averages $2,200 to $3,400 per person based on double occupancy. Budget travelers can complete the route for $700 to $1,000 using public buses and basic guesthouses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08); padding: 18px 0;">
          <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; color: #111;">Is it better to rent a car or hire a private driver for 10 days in Morocco?</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Hiring a private driver is strongly recommended for a 10-day circuit. Mountain passes like Tizi n'Tichka feature steep drop-offs, hairpin turns, heavy truck traffic, and unpredictable weather. A licensed local driver manages mountain driving, police checkpoints, medina parking, and luggage security while sharing native cultural context.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08); padding: 18px 0;">
          <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; color: #111;">Can you visit both Marrakech, Fes, and the Sahara Desert in 10 days?</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Yes, this 10-day circuit is specifically designed around Marrakech, Fes, and Merzouga (Erg Chebbi). Breaking the drive between Marrakech and Merzouga with an overnight in the Dades Valley keeps daily driving to a manageable 4 to 6 hours while providing full days in both imperial capitals.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08); padding: 18px 0;">
          <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; color: #111;">When is the best time of year for a 10-day Morocco trip?</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Spring (March to May) and autumn (September to November) offer optimal weather across all regions. Temperatures in Marrakech and Fes average 22°C to 28°C (72°F-82°F), mountain passes are clear of snow, and desert nights in Erg Chebbi are comfortably cool rather than freezing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="padding: 18px 0;">
          <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; color: #111;">What should I pack and wear for 10 days in Morocco?</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Pack lightweight, breathable natural fabrics (linen, cotton) that cover shoulders and knees for respect in ancient medinas and rural villages. Bring comfortable, closed-toe walking shoes for cobblestones, a warm fleece or down jacket for cold desert nights, sunglasses, high-SPF sunscreen, and a scarf or cheche for sand protection in Erg Chebbi.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

